  "summary": "A boiler feedwater pump raises water from the deaerator (low pressure, near saturation temperature) to above boiler drum or steam-generator pressure — often well over 100 bar in power plants. To reach that head at reasonable speed it uses several impellers in series: after each impeller a diffuser recovers pressure and return vanes lead the water inward to the next impeller eye. The stage casings are clamped between suction and discharge casings by tie bolts (ring-section design). A balance drum at the high-pressure end cancels most of the axial thrust. A minimum-flow recirculation valve protects the pump at low boiler demand.",
  "classification": {
    "family": "Rotodynamic (centrifugal), radial flow, multistage",
    "configuration": "Horizontal, between bearings, ring-section (segmental), radially split",
    "api_610_type": "BB4 (single casing, radially split, multistage); barrel BB5 for highest pressures"
  },
  "model_configuration": {
    "stages": 5,
    "axial_thrust": "Balance drum (balance disc or opposed impellers are alternatives)",
    "driver": "Motor via flexible spacer coupling (turbine or VFD/hydraulic coupling on large units)",
    "npsh_note": "Deaerator elevation or a booster pump provides NPSH margin for hot feedwater."
  },
